     **Bug[0m[2m 1: Duplicate[0m[2m processing**[0m[2m
     
     In `dequeue[0m[2m()`, there[0m[2m's a race[0m[2m condition.[0m[2m When multiple workers call[0m[2m `dequeue()`[0m[2m concurrently:[0m[2m
     
     ```[0m[2mpython
     async[0m[2m def dequeue(self)[0m[2m -> Optional[Dict[0m[2m]:
         for job[0m[2m in self._jobs[0m[2m.values():
             if[0m[2m job["status"][0m[2m == "pending":[0m[2m
                 await asyncio.sleep[0m[2m(0.01[0m[2m)  # Y[0m[2mIELDS[0m[2m CONTROL[0m[2m HERE
                 job[0m[2m["status"] =[0m[2m "processing"[0m[2m
                 return job[0m[2m
         return None
     ``[0m[2m`
     
     The problem[0m[2m:[0m[2m Between checking `job[0m[2m["status"] ==[0m[2m "pending"`[0m[2m and setting it[0m[2m to `"[0m[2mprocessing"[0m[2m`, there's an[0m[2m `await asyncio.sleep[0m[2m(0.01[0m[2m)`. When[0m[2m a[0m[2m coroutine hits[0m[2m `await`, it[0m[2m yields control to[0m[2m the[0m[2m event loop, allowing[0m[2m another[0m[2m coroutine to also[0m[2m see[0m[2m the same job[0m[2m as "pending"[0m[2m and pick[0m[2m it up. This[0m[2m is a[0m[2m classic TOCTOU[0m[2m (time-of-check[0m[2m-to[0m[2m-time-of-use[0m[2m) race[0m[2m condition.
     
     The[0m[2m fix: Remove[0m[2m the sleep[0m[2m ([0m[2mit seems[0m[2m to[0m[2m simulate[0m[2m some[0m[2m latency[0m[2m,[0m[2m but it's causing[0m[2m the race condition),[0m[2m or better yet,[0m[2m set the status to[0m[2m "processing" immediately[0m[2m before any[0m[2m await, so[0m[2m no[0m[2m other coroutine[0m[2m can pick it[0m[2m up.
     
     Actually,[0m[2m looking more carefully,[0m[2m the `await[0m[2m asyncio.sleep(0[0m[2m.01)` is[0m[2m explicitly[0m[2m creating[0m[2m a race condition window[0m[2m. The[0m[2m fix should be to[0m[2m atom[0m[2mically mark[0m[2m the job as "[0m[2mprocessing" before yielding[0m[2m.[0m[2m We[0m[2m should set[0m[2m the status to[0m[2m "processing" before[0m[2m the `await`,[0m[2m not after.[0m[2m
     
     But[0m[2m wait - we[0m[2m also[0m[2m need to be[0m[2m careful.[0m[2m Even[0m[2m without[0m[2m the sleep, in[0m[2m pure[0m[2m asyncio ([0m[2mno[0m[2m threading[0m[2m), there wouldn[0m[2m't be a race[0m[2m condition unless[0m[2m there's an[0m[2m `await` between[0m[2m the check and the[0m[2m update[0m[2m. The[0m[2m `[0m[2masync[0m[2mio[0m[2m.sleep(0.[0m[2m01)` is the[0m[2m only[0m[2m yield point,[0m[2m and[0m[2m it's placed[0m[2m between the check and[0m[2m the update,[0m[2m which creates[0m[2m the race.
     
     The[0m[2m simplest fix: move[0m[2m `[0m[2mjob["status"][0m[2m = "processing"`[0m[2m before the `await[0m[2m`, or[0m[2m remove the sleep entirely[0m[2m.[0m[2m
     
     Actually, the clean[0m[2mest fix that[0m[2m preserves the intent[0m[2m (sim[0m[2mulating some async[0m[2m work during[0m[2m dequeue) is to[0m[2m mark[0m[2m the job as processing[0m[2m immediately upon[0m[2m finding it, before[0m[2m any await:
     
     ```[0m[2mpython
